This reminds me of APL code and is just hard to read and understand. 

I would recommend doing this in two steps:

1. Check if the `sfFirstNFTokenSequence` is present and if it is not then **set** it.
2. Read the `sfFirstNFTokenSequence` using `(*root)[sfFirstNFTokenSequence]` at the point of use, confident that it will be there because you just made sure it would be.


Consider this lambda:

```
// ***WARNING*** UNTESTED CODE; I JUST TYPED IT UP AS AN EXAMPLE
auto const tokenSeq = [this, &issuer]() -> Expected<std::uint32_t, TER> {
    auto const root = view().peek(keylet::account(issuer));

    // Should not happen.  Checked in preclaim.
    if (root == nullptr)
        return Unexpected(tecNO_ISSUER);

    if (!ctx_.view().rules().enabled(fixUnburnableNFToken))
    {
        // Get the unique sequence number for this token and detect
        // possible overflow:
        auto const seq = (*root)[~sfMintedNFTokens].value_or(0);

        (*root)[sfMintedNFTokens] = seq + 1;

        if ((*root)[sfMintedNFTokens] == 0)
            return Unexpected(tecMAX_SEQUENCE_REACHED);

        ctx_.view().update(root);
        return seq;
    }

    // If the issuer hasn't minted a NFT before, we must
    // initialize sfFirstNFTokenSequence to equal to the
    // current account sequence.
    if (auto fts = (*root)[~sfFirstNFTokenSequence]; !fts)
    {
        // NOTICE: I don't subtract by one here; the logic is that the
        // precise value of this field doesn't _really_ matter as long
        // as it's guaranteed to be different from values that deleted
        // accounts had in the past.
        (*root)[sfFirstNFTokenSequence] = (*root)[sfSequence];
    }

    auto const mintcnt = (*root)[~sfMintedNFTokens].value_or(0);

    (*root)[sfMintedNFTokens] = mintcnt + 1;

    if ((*root)[sfMintedNFTokens] == 0)
        return Unexpected(tecMAX_SEQUENCE_REACHED);

    auto const offset = (*root)[sfFirstNFTokenSequence];
    auto const seq = mintcnt + offset;

    // It's probably overkill and unlikely to ever trigger, but do some
    // additional overflow detection here:
    if (seq < mintcnt || seq < offset)
        return Unexpected(tecMAX_SEQUENCE_REACHED);

    return seq;
}();
```

Triple-check this code _BEFORE_ using it. I just typed it up in the GitHub comment box so it could be wrong. Notice that I *rework* the logic for the non-amendment path; the "result" should be identical, but triple check that too (or, better yet, use the original version of that code).

re:
```
        // NOTICE: I don't subtract by one here; the logic is that the
        // precise value of this field doesn't _really_ matter as long
        // as it's guaranteed to be different from values that deleted
        // accounts had in the past.
```

The precise value of the NFTokenID (specifically NFT sequence) does not matter in real world. But it needs to be precise and deterministic because of how test cases generate and test NFTokenIDs (in [token::getNextID(...)](https://github.com/XRPLF/rippled/blob/4a26e808b1e3e311e391530f09c7d5adc242a31d/src/test/jtx/impl/token.cpp#L61))

Without subtracting one in cases of authorized minting and tickets, the NFTokenIDs generated in tests will sometimes be off-by-one

You have the right behavior, but I don't think the reasoning is correct.  I suggest rewriting the comments and code like this:
```
        // With fixNFTokenRemint amendment enabled:
        //
        // If the issuer hasn't minted an NFToken before we must add a
        // FirstNFTokenSequence field to the issuer's AccountRoot.  The
        // value of the FirstNFTokenSequence must equal the issuer's
        // current account sequence.
        //
        // There are three situations:
        //  o If the first token is being minted by the issuer and
        //     * If the transaction consumes a Sequence number, then the
        //       Sequence has been pre-incremented by the time we get here in
        //       doApply.  We must decrement the value in the Sequence field.
        //     * Otherwise the transaction uses a Ticket so the Sequence has
        //       not been pre-incremented.  We use the Sequence value as is.
        //  o The first token is being minted by an authorized minter.  In
        //    this case the issuer's Sequence field has been left untouched.
        //    We use the issuer's Sequence value as is.
        if (!root->isFieldPresent(sfFirstNFTokenSequence))
        {
            auto const accSeq = root->at(sfSequence);

            root->at(sfFirstNFTokenSequence) =
                ctx_.tx.isFieldPresent(sfIssuer) ||
                    ctx_.tx.getSeqProxy().isTicket()
                ? accSeq
                : accSeq - 1;
        }
```

This works, but it leaves `tokenSeq` uninitialized which is not the safest choice.  The code can be simplified a bit like this:
```
                std::uint32_t tokenSeq =
                    env.le(acct)->at(~sfMintedNFTokens).value_or(0);

                // If fixNFTokenRemint amendment is on, we must
                // add FirstNFTokenSequence.
                if (env.current()->rules().enabled(fixNFTokenRemint))
                    tokenSeq += env.le(acct)
                                    ->at(~sfFirstNFTokenSequence)
                                    .value_or(env.seq(acct));
```

This is a great comment!  Thanks for explaining the motivation.

Since the test works correctly with the `close()` moved outside the loop in one case, then we don't need the `close()` inside of the loop at all. But I don't want to lose the comment.  So my suggestion would be to remove the `if ... close()` altogether.  The comment might look like this now:
```
                    // Do not close the ledger inside the loop.  If
                    // fixNFTokenRemint is enabled and accounts are initialized
                    // at different ledgers, they will have different account
                    // sequences.  That would cause the accounts to have
                    // different NFTokenID sequence numbers.
```

This works, but it leaves `tokenSeq` uninitialized which is not the safest choice.  The code can be simplified a bit like this:
```
    std::uint32_t nftSeq = env.le(issuer)->at(~sfMintedNFTokens).value_or(0);

    // If fixNFTokenRemint amendment is on, we must add FirstNFTokenSequence.
    if (env.current()->rules().enabled(fixNFTokenRemint))
        nftSeq += env.le(issuer)
                      ->at(~sfFirstNFTokenSequence)
                      .value_or(env.seq(issuer));
```

There are a number of changes like this throughout this file.  I think it would make the tests easier to read if the entire `if (features[fixNFTokenRemint))` thing were tucked into `token::getID()`.

How could you do that?  An easy and consistent way to do that would be pass `env` to `token::getID()`.  If you did that then the implementation of `token::getID()` might look like this:
```
uint256
getID(
    jtx::Env const& env,
    jtx::Account const& issuer,
    std::uint32_t nfTokenTaxon,
    std::uint32_t nftSeq,
    std::uint16_t flags,
    std::uint16_t xferFee)
{
    if (env.current()->rules().enabled(fixNFTokenRemint))
    {
        // If fixNFTokenRemint is enabled, we must add issuer's
        // FirstNFTokenSequence to offset the starting NFT sequence number.
        nftSeq += env.le(issuer)
                      ->at(~sfFirstNFTokenSequence)
                      .value_or(env.seq(issuer));
    }
    return ripple::NFTokenMint::createNFTokenID(
        flags, xferFee, issuer, nft::toTaxon(nfTokenTaxon), nftSeq);
}
```
If you make that change then `token::getID()` can be handled consistently throughout this entire file.

If you change `token::getID()` you'll also want to fix the `token::getNextID()` implementation like this:
```
uint256
getNextID(
    jtx::Env const& env,
    jtx::Account const& issuer,
    std::uint32_t nfTokenTaxon,
    std::uint16_t flags,
    std::uint16_t xferFee)
{
    std::uint32_t const nftSeq =
        env.le(issuer)->at(~sfMintedNFTokens).value_or(0);
    return token::getID(env, issuer, nfTokenTaxon, nftSeq, flags, xferFee);
}
```

